--- body ---
## Context
R35 entered the sheet on 2026-08-31 with an `inferred` standing row, which is
the weakest evidence class: neither measured nor read at source. The promise is
that a new, changed or deleted item becomes visible to the index inside the
discovery bound. Two things are missing and they are different jobs.

What upstream does. Incremental updates ride a library version cursor and
deletion reconciles against the key set, so the machinery exists. Nobody here
has established what *triggers* a run, or how often — whether the update path
is a cadence, a hook on a Zotero event, or something that only happens when a
build or a query asks. Until that is read at source the row cannot leave
`inferred`, and R35's standing is a guess in either direction.

What the bound is. SPEC.md §5.2.4 states a reconcile cadence and a disclosed
deletion latency, and R35 delegates its value there on R6's pattern. Whether
those two numbers are the right promise to a user is not settled: they were
chosen as design, before any requirement pointed at them.

## Exit criteria
- [x] the upstream trigger and cadence read at source at the reviewed SHA, with
      the file and line recorded here
- [ ] deletion latency and new-item latency measured on the reference machine,
      artifact committed under `bench/results/`, with each tick's `operation`
      recorded and the delta and full-rebuild distributions reported apart —
      `action:"update"` falls back to a full rebuild on six conditions
      (`src/features/search/build.ts:380-388` @ `b05ed69`), so a single mean
      over an unrecorded mix is not a measurement
- [x] R35's row moves off `inferred`, or says why it cannot
- [ ] the §2.4 values are confirmed as the bound or re-pinned from the
      measurement, with the ruling in DECISIONS.md if they move

## Actions
1. Build `fork/` at the CURRENT `UPSTREAM_REVIEWED_SHA` (`make upstream-checkout`;
   re-read `UPSTREAM`, do not copy a SHA from this ticket's log — it moves, and
   already moved once during this ticket's life, see the 2026-09-03T18:00Z
   drift-guard entry). Before trusting any file:line citation below, re-derive
   it at the SHA actually built if `UPSTREAM` has moved again.
2. Target padme: live Zotero desktop already synced, local API at
   `http://127.0.0.1:23119/api/users/0/`, datadir
   `/home/haduong/data/Zotero-fresh`. Confirm idle first — no build in
   flight, `localApiDegradedAt` unset — the same precondition doudou could
   not offer this session.
3. One-time manual step: Zotero 10+ gates local-API writes behind an in-app
   grant dialog (`POST /api/local/authorize`, `src/api/local-writes.ts`).
   Someone must click "Always Allow" once in padme's desktop app before any
   scripted add/delete can run. Not automatable — flag it, don't script
   around it.
4. Reuse `bench/mcp_drive.py`'s `Server` class to drive the built fork over
   stdio — the same harness the ticket 0520 live-smoke recipe used (memory
   `project-live-smoke-recipe`). Do not hand-roll a new MCP client or timer.
5. Establish the delta-path precondition before measuring anything: run
   `zotero_index action:"build"` once against the SQLite backend
   (`supportsDelete=true`), poll `action:"status"` to `state:"done"`, and
   confirm the resulting status carries a `libraryVersion` stamp and an
   embedder identity matching the running config. A completed same-backend,
   same-embedder SQLite build satisfies four of `updateBlocker`'s six
   conditions by construction (readable store, delete-capable store,
   non-empty index, stamped); the remaining two (backend mismatch, embedder
   mismatch) are avoided by never switching either between build and update.
6. Deletion latency: create one throwaway test item via the local API
   (never delete a real library item), then delete it. Record delete time
   `t0`. Call `action:"update"`; poll `action:"status"` and read
   `s.operation` off every poll (per `index-tool.ts`'s own
   `s.operation === 'update' ? 'Index update' : … 'Full index rebuild'`
   pattern) to label the tick delta or rebuild. Poll `/fulltext` (or query)
   until the deleted item's text is no longer served; record `t1`. Latency
   = `t1 - t0`.
7. New-item latency: add one throwaway test item via the local API. Record
   add time `t0`. Call `action:"update"`, poll status/operation as above.
   Poll the search tool until the new item is discoverable. Record `t2`.
   Latency = `t2 - t0`.
8. Repeat both (add, delete) for N ≥ 5 reps. Log `operation` per tick.
   Partition into delta-tick vs full-rebuild-tick distributions — never
   average across the two. A rep that unexpectedly triggers a full rebuild
   stays in its own bucket rather than being discarded, per the
   2026-09-02T21:04Z finding this ticket exists to honor.
9. Record the item-census cost (SPEC.md §5.2.4 item 1/3 — the
   `?since=item_watermark` fetch plus deletion-by-subtraction) separately
   from the full-text census cost SPEC.md already reports (~8,037 entries).
   This is the number SPEC.md §5.2.4 itself calls "unmeasured, unlike the
   full-text one above".
10. Commit the artifact under `bench/results/0503-discovery-latency/` (raw
    timings + operation labels + a short README stating machine, item
    count, and library scope — personal-only or personal+groups, see the
    item-count-scope flag in the log).
11. Confirm or re-pin SPEC.md §5.2.4's 60 s bound: if measured update-run
    cost plus item-census-per-tick cost does not fit inside 60 s with
    margin, that is a finding about the *cadence*, not grounds to
    relitigate R35's one-minute number (already ruled, 2026-08-31). Record
    a DECISIONS.md ruling only if the §5.2.4 cadence number itself moves.

## Test
Red step first: before trusting the classifier on real reps, force a known
full rebuild (`action:"update"` immediately after `action:"refresh"` —
condition 3, empty index — is guaranteed to hit the fallback) and confirm the
harness's operation-reading correctly labels it "Full index rebuild", not
"update". This is the positive control for the delta/rebuild split; without
it a harness that always reports "delta" would pass silently on every rep.

## Verification
- [ ] fork built at the CURRENT `UPSTREAM_REVIEWED_SHA`, re-read from
      `UPSTREAM` at run time, not copied from this ticket's log
- [ ] padme's local API confirmed idle before the run (no build in flight)
- [ ] local-API write grant obtained (one-time interactive step, recorded
      in the artifact's README)
- [ ] positive control (forced full rebuild) correctly classified before
      the real reps are trusted
- [ ] every rep's `operation` field recorded; delta and full-rebuild reps
      reported as separate distributions, never averaged into one mean
- [ ] item-count scope (personal-only vs personal+groups) stated in the
      artifact
- [ ] artifact committed under `bench/results/`
- [ ] SPEC.md §5.2.4's bound confirmed or re-pinned, with a DECISIONS.md
      ruling only if it moves

## Invariants
- Never delete or modify a real item in padme's synced library — every
  add/delete in the test uses throwaway items created for the purpose.
- Never drive padme's local API while another lane is mid-build on it —
  the same contention that deferred this half on doudou.
- The delta and full-rebuild distributions are reported apart; a script
  that only ever reports a combined mean has not met this ticket's exit
  criterion regardless of how many reps it ran.

